   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "On 11/19/2015 12:24 PM, Daniel wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt; I&#39;m starting to use lavaan for longitudinal data analysis and looked at\r<br>&gt; the tutorials at <a href=\"http://lavaan.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lavaan.org&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429269250000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0ZX4NgA3lPq94zPBMcVsZd\">lavaan.org</a>\r<br>&gt; &lt;<a href=\"http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/growth.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/growth.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429269250000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3aMC99KFx9qruAzzObeOCR\">http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/growth.html</a>&gt;. I wonder, if it is always\r<br>&gt; the case that the intercept has a &quot;1 *...&quot;\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; |i =~1*t1 +1*t2 +1*t3 +1*t4|\r<br>\r<br>Yes.\r<br>\r<br>&gt; while the slope has increasing values for each time point.\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; |s =~0*t1 +1*t2 +2*t3 +3*t4|\r<br>\r<br>For a linear slope, you need to have (linearly) increasing numbers, \r<br>although you can play around with the 0; you can also have something \r<br>like -2 -1 0 1\r<br>\r<br>For each timepoint, following your 0/1/2/3 sequence, you assume the \r<br>following (linear) trend for the 4 timepoints:\r<br>\r<br>E(t1) = 1*mean_i + 0*mean_s\r<br>E(t2) = 1*mean_i + 1*mean_s\r<br>E(t3) = 1*mean_i + 2*mean_s\r<br>E(t4) = 1*mean_i + 3*mean_s\r<br>\r<br>where &#39;mean_i&#39; is the mean of the random intercept, and &#39;mean_s&#39; is the \r<br>mean of the random slope.\r<br>\r<br>Yves.\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
